-- src:1878 | (17) During the pendency of such judicial review, or any appeal therefrom, the administrative action under review shall be and remain in full force and effect, unless on application to the court on not less than ten days’ notice, and after hearing thereon and a consideration by the court of the applicant’s likelihood of prevailing on the merits and of irreparable injury, the court temporarily stays such administrative action pending disposition of such trial or appeal.
@ref s 2023(a)(17) during judicial review the administrative action remains in full force and effect unless the court temporarily stays it after notice, hearing and consideration (src:1878)
-- In plain words: while the court case or an appeal is pending the action stays in force. The court can stay it only on application on at least ten days' notice, after a hearing, and after considering the applicant's likelihood of prevailing on the merits and of irreparable injury.
GIVEN `judicial review or an appeal is pending` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court has temporarily stayed the administrative action pending disposition of the trial or appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the number of days of notice given on the application to the court` IS A NUMBER
      `a hearing has been held on the application` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court has considered the applicant's likelihood of prevailing on the merits and of irreparable injury` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2023(a)(17) the administrative action remains in full force and effect` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `judicial review or an appeal is pending` THEN
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "During the pendency of such judicial review, or any appeal therefrom"
            NOTHING
        IF `the court has temporarily stayed the administrative action pending disposition of the trial or appeal` AND `the number of days of notice given on the application to the court` AT LEAST 10 AND `a hearing has been held on the application` AND `the court has considered the applicant's likelihood of prevailing on the merits and of irreparable injury` THEN
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless on application to the court on not less than ten days' notice, and after hearing thereon and a consideration by the court of the applicant's likelihood of prevailing on the merits and of irreparable injury, the court temporarily stays such administrative action"
            JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:1879 | (18) Suspension of stores pending review.—
